`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"What the NoPE evidence covers",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Haviv et al. ask whether causal language models trained without an explicit positional encoding still learn where each token is. A probe tests this. It is trained on a model's hidden states to predict each token's absolute position, and if it succeeds, the states hold that information. In the models the authors study, probes recover absolute position ",e.jsx(a,{id:"haviv2022",children:"Haviv et al., 2022"}),". They offer the growing prefix as a possible explanation, since it lets a model count predecessors. The study identifies no circuit that every causal decoder uses. It also does not show recovery at lengths the models have not seen."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Kazemnejad et al. ask which positional scheme generalizes best from shorter training sequences to longer evaluation sequences. On ten controlled algorithmic and reasoning tasks, with 100M-parameter decoder-only models, NoPE does best in their comparison ",e.jsx(a,{id:"kazemnejad2023",children:"Kazemnejad et al., 2023"}),". The result holds for those tasks, that scale and that training. It does not show that NoPE matches the other schemes in frontier models at the training length."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The exercise adds a third kind of evidence. It trains a ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"nope"})," arm beside the RoPE default and measures training loss at one sequence length, one step budget and one model scale. Report those three with the result, and keep the claim to the comparison you measured."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Hybrids mix RoPE and NoPE across layers",reading:!0,accent:2,number:"32",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
