`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"OV circuit: decides what gets written back"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Heads can mix several behaviors"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Heads compose across layers, as induction heads show ",e.jsx(a,{id:"olsson2022",children:"Olsson et al., 2022"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Two circuits as a way to analyze a head",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Elhage et al. analyze an attention head as two circuits ",e.jsx(a,{id:"elhage2021",children:"Elhage et al., 2021"}),". The ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"QK circuit"}),", formed by the query and key projections, sets the attention scores, and so which keys a query attends to. The ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"OV circuit"}),", formed by the value and output projections, decides how the attended information is transformed before it is written back into the residual stream."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Without a position rotation, each circuit of head ",e.jsx(s,{math:"h",display:!1})," is one ",e.jsx(s,{math:"D \\times D",display:!1})," matrix. The QK circuit is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_Q^{(h)} W_K^{(h)\\top}",display:!1,trail:","})," and the OV circuit is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_V^{(h)} W_O^{(h)}",display:!1,trail:","})," where ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_O^{(h)}",display:!1})," is the block of rows of ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_O",display:!1})," that multiplies the head's context. The QK circuit enters only the attention weights, so it chooses where the head reads. The OV circuit acts on whatever those weights select, so it sets what the read contributes."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The circuits describe a head's computation, which need not amount to one job a person can name, because a head can combine several behaviors. The output projection ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_O",display:!1})," also mixes the contributions of all heads, and later layers can build on what earlier heads wrote."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Depth lets heads in different layers compose. One studied example is the induction pattern: when a sequence contains ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[A][B]"})," and later ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[A]"})," again, the model predicts ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B]"}),". A previous-token head and an induction head in a later layer produce this pattern together ",e.jsx(a,{id:"olsson2022",children:"Olsson et al., 2022"}),". It is one studied mechanism, not a claim about every head. The previous-token head writes into each position which token came before it. The induction head at the second ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[A]"})," compares its query with those writes and attends to the position whose previous token was ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[A]"}),", which is the position of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B]"}),". Its OV circuit then copies ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B]"})," toward the prediction."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The exercise does not ask you to find or build an induction head. Finding the circuits in the heads of a small trained model would make a good final project."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{optional:!0,title:"NoPE after post-training (Qwen3.8)",accent:6,number:"37",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
